#: ../../../sensors.md:91
msgid "Contact sensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:93
msgid ""
"Let's introduce a different type of sensor, the `contact` sensor. You can "
"guess from the name that this sensor gives indication when it touches "
"(contacts) something else. We will build an obstacle (wall) and add the "
"contact sensor to it. If the robot hits the obstacle it will stop, "
"preventing the robot from damaging itself. Let's first build the obstacle as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:100
msgid ""
"<model name='wall'>\n"
"    <static>true</static>\n"
"    <pose>5 0 0 0 0 0</pose><!--pose relative to the world-->\n"
"    <link name='box'>\n"
"        <visual name='visual'>\n"
"            <geometry>\n"
"                <box>\n"
"                    <size>0.5 10.0 2.0</size>\n"
"                </box>\n"
"            </geometry>\n"
"            <!--let's add color to our link-->\n"
"            <material>\n"
"                <ambient>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</ambient>\n"
"                <diffuse>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</diffuse>\n"
"                <specular>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</specular>\n"
"            </material>\n"
"        </visual>\n"
"        <collision name='collision'>\n"
"            <geometry>\n"
"                <box>\n"
"                    <size>0.5 10.0 2.0</size>\n"
"                </box>\n"
"            </geometry>\n"
"        </collision>\n"
"    </link>\n"
"</model>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:129
msgid ""
"It is just a simple model with one link of box shape. You can check the "
"[Build your own robot tutorial](building_robot) to learn how to build models."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:131
msgid ""
"Now run the world and make sure that the wall appears in the simulation like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:133
msgid "![wall_in_world](tutorials/sensors/sensor_wall.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:133
msgid "wall_in_world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:135
msgid ""
"Let's add the contact sensor to the wall. As with the `IMU` sensor, we "
"should first define the `Contact` sensor by adding the following code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:137
msgid ""
"<plugin filename=\"gz-sim-contact-system\"\n"
"        name=\"gz::sim::systems::Contact\">\n"
"</plugin>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:143
msgid ""
"Now we can add the `contact` sensor to the `box` link of the `wall` model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:145
msgid ""
"<sensor name='sensor_contact' type='contact'>\n"
"    <contact>\n"
"        <collision>collision</collision>\n"
"    </contact>\n"
"</sensor>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:153
msgid ""
"The definition of the `<sensor>` is straight forward, we just define the "
"`name` and the `type` of the sensor. And inside the `collision` we define "
"the box link collision name which is `collision`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:155
msgid ""
"We need also to add the `TouchPlugin` under the `wall` model as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:157
msgid ""
"<plugin filename=\"gz-sim-touchplugin-system\"\n"
"        name=\"gz::sim::systems::TouchPlugin\">\n"
"    <target>vehicle_blue</target>\n"
"    <namespace>wall</namespace>\n"
"    <time>0.001</time>\n"
"    <enabled>true</enabled>\n"
"</plugin>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:167
msgid ""
"The `TouchPlugin` will publish (send) a message when the `wall` has been "
"touched. The tags of the plugin are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:170
msgid ""
"`<target>` which will be in contact with our wall, in our case "
"`vehicle_blue`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:171
msgid ""
"`<namespace>` takes the namespace of our topic, so when our robot hits the "
"wall it will send a message to `/wall/touched` topic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:173
msgid "Run the world in one terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:177
msgid "In another terminal, listen to the `/wall/touched` topic:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:179
msgid "`gz topic -e -t /wall/touched`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:181
msgid ""
"Drive your robot forward to the wall using the keyboard arrow keys. Make "
"sure to start the simulation by hitting the play button, and enable the Key "
"Publisher plugin as well by clicking on the plugins dropdown list (vertical "
"ellipsis), then selecting \"Key Publisher\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:183
msgid ""
"When you hit the bump you should see a message `data: true` on the terminal "
"where you ran the `gz topic -e -t /wall/touched`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:185
msgid ""
"Now we can use the `TriggeredPublisher` plugin to make our robot stop when "
"hits the wall as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:187
msgid ""
"<plugin filename=\"gz-sim-triggered-publisher-system\"\n"
"        name=\"gz::sim::systems::TriggeredPublisher\">\n"
"    <input type=\"gz.msgs.Boolean\" topic=\"/wall/touched\">\n"
"        <match>data: true</match>\n"
"    </input>\n"
"    <output type=\"gz.msgs.Twist\" topic=\"/cmd_vel\">\n"
"        linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: 0.0}\n"
"    </output>\n"
"</plugin>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:199
msgid ""
"As explained in the [Moving robot tutorial](moving_robot), we can publish an "
"output depending on a received input. So when we receive `data: true` on the "
"`/wall/touched` topic we publish `linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: 0.0}` to "
"make our robot stop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:204
msgid "Lidar sensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:206
msgid ""
"We don't want our robot to touch the wall at all because this may cause some "
"damage, so instead of the contact sensor we can use the Lidar. Lidar is an "
"acronym for \"light detection and ranging\". This sensor can help us detect "
"obstacles around the robot. We will use it to measure the distance between "
"our robot and the wall."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:208
msgid ""
"First let's create a frame to fix our lidar to. This should be added inside "
"of the `vehicle_blue` `<model>` tag, since the lidar frame is attached to "
"the robot's `chassis`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:210
msgid ""
"<frame name=\"lidar_frame\" attached_to='chassis'>\n"
"    <pose>0.8 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"</frame>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:216
msgid ""
"Then add this plugin under the `<world>` tag, to be able to use the `lidar` "
"sensor:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:218
msgid ""
"    <plugin\n"
"      filename=\"gz-sim-sensors-system\"\n"
"      name=\"gz::sim::systems::Sensors\">\n"
"      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>\n"
"    </plugin>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:226
msgid "Under the `chassis` link we can add the `lidar` sensor as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:228
msgid ""
"<sensor name='gpu_lidar' type='gpu_lidar'>\"\n"
"    <pose relative_to='lidar_frame'>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"    <topic>lidar</topic>\n"
"    <update_rate>10</update_rate>\n"
"    <ray>\n"
"        <scan>\n"
"            <horizontal>\n"
"                <samples>640</samples>\n"
"                <resolution>1</resolution>\n"
"                <min_angle>-1.396263</min_angle>\n"
"                <max_angle>1.396263</max_angle>\n"
"            </horizontal>\n"
"            <vertical>\n"
"                <samples>1</samples>\n"
"                <resolution>0.01</resolution>\n"
"                <min_angle>0</min_angle>\n"
"                <max_angle>0</max_angle>\n"
"            </vertical>\n"
"        </scan>\n"
"        <range>\n"
"            <min>0.08</min>\n"
"            <max>10.0</max>\n"
"            <resolution>0.01</resolution>\n"
"        </range>\n"
"    </ray>\n"
"    <always_on>1</always_on>\n"
"    <visualize>true</visualize>\n"
"</sensor>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:259
msgid ""
"First we defined the `name` and `type` of our sensor, then we defined its "
"`<pose>` relative to the `lidar_frame`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:261
msgid ""
"In the `<topic>` we define the topic on which the lidar data will be "
"published."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:262
msgid ""
"`<update_rate>` is the frequency at which the `lidar` data is generated, in "
"our case `10 Hz` which is equal to `0.1 sec`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:264
msgid ""
"Under the `<horizontal>` and `<vertical>` tags we define the properties of "
"the horizontal and vertical laser rays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:266
msgid ""
"`<samples>` is the number of simulated lidar rays to generate per complete "
"laser sweep cycle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:268
msgid ""
"`<resolution>`: this number is multiplied by samples to determine the number "
"range data points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:270
msgid ""
"The `<min_angle>` and `<max_angle>` are the angle range of the generated "
"rays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:271
msgid "Under the `<range>` we define range properties of each simulated ray"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:272
msgid ""
"`<min>` and `<max>` define the minimum and maximum distance for each lidar "
"ray."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:273
msgid ""
"The `<resolution>` tag here defines the linear resolution of each lidar ray."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:274
msgid ""
"`<always_on>`: if true the sensor will always be updated according to the "
"`<update_rate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:275
msgid "`<visualize>`: if true the sensor is visualized in the GUI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:277
msgid "Now run the world and press the play button in the bottom-left corner:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:281
msgid ""
"Look at the lidar messages on the `/lidar` topic, specifically the `ranges` "
"data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:283
msgid "`gz topic -e -t /lidar`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:285
msgid "The lidar message has the following attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:287
msgid ""
"message LaserScan\n"
"{\n"
"  Header header              = 1;\n"
"\n"
"  string frame               = 2;\n"
"  Pose world_pose            = 3;\n"
"  double angle_min           = 4;\n"
"  double angle_max           = 5;\n"
"  double angle_step          = 6;\n"
"  double range_min           = 7;\n"
"  double range_max           = 8;\n"
"  uint32 count               = 9;\n"
"  double vertical_angle_min  = 10;\n"
"  double vertical_angle_max  = 11;\n"
"  double vertical_angle_step = 12;\n"
"  uint32 vertical_count      = 13;\n"
"\n"
"  repeated double ranges              = 14;\n"
"  repeated double intensities         = 15;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:310
msgid "Avoid the wall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:312
msgid ""
"Now as we have the lidar on our robot, we can use the `ranges` data and make "
"our robot avoid hitting the wall. To do that, we'll write a short C++ "
"program that listens to the sensor data and sends velocity commands to the "
"robot. This program is called a node. We will build a node that subscribes "
"to the `/lidar` topic and reads its data. Have a look at this [tutorial]"
"(https://gazebosim.org/api/transport/15/messages.html) to learn how to build "
"a `publisher` and a `subscriber` node. You can download the finished node "
"for this demo from [here](https://github.com/gazebosim/docs/blob/master/"
"jetty/tutorials/sensors/lidar_node.cc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:322
msgid "The lidar_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:324
msgid ""
"std::string topic_pub = \"/cmd_vel\";\n"
"gz::transport::Node node;\n"
"auto pub = node.Advertise<gz::msgs::Twist>(topic_pub);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:330
msgid ""
"We declare a `node` which will publish to `cmd_vel` topic. Then advertise "
"our node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:332
msgid ""
"void cb(const gz::msgs::LaserScan &_msg)\n"
"{\n"
"  gz::msgs::Twist data;\n"
"\n"
"  bool allMore = true;\n"
"  for (int i = 0; i < _msg.ranges_size(); i++)\n"
"  {\n"
"    if (_msg.ranges(i) < 1.0)\n"
"    {\n"
"      allMore = false;\n"
"      break;\n"
"    }\n"
"  }\n"
"  if (allMore) //if all bigger than one\n"
"  {\n"
"    data.mutable_linear()->set_x(0.5);\n"
"    data.mutable_angular()->set_z(0.0);\n"
"  }\n"
"  else\n"
"  {\n"
"    data.mutable_linear()->set_x(0.0);\n"
"    data.mutable_angular()->set_z(0.5);\n"
"  }\n"
"  pub.Publish(data);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:360
msgid ""
"Inside the callback function we check if the range of all rays are bigger "
"than `1.0`. If so we publish a message to our car to move forward. Otherwise "
"we make the robot rotate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:363
msgid ""
"int main(int argc, char **argv)\n"
"{\n"
"    std::string topic_sub = \"/lidar\";\n"
"    // Subscribe to a topic by registering a callback.\n"
"    if (!node.Subscribe(topic_sub, cb))\n"
"    {\n"
"        std::cerr << \"Error subscribing to topic [\" << topic_sub << \"]\" "
"<< std::endl;\n"
"        return -1;\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    // Zzzzzz.\n"
"    gz::transport::waitForShutdown();\n"
"\n"
"    return 0;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../sensors.md:381
msgid ""
"Inside the main we subscribe to the `lidar` topic, and wait until the node "
"is shut down."
msgstr ""

